@meta
id: 20-19
title: Put by line number
chapter: 20
type: lesson
skills: register-named
par: 54
minutes: 5
vscode: cut, Ctrl-Home, paste, cut, Ctrl-End, paste
open: registry.ts

@brief
`p` puts relative to the cursor. `:put` puts relative to a line
number, and it always puts whole lines, whatever shape the register
is. `:0put a` puts register `a` above the first line. `:$put a` puts it
after the last. `:put` on its own uses the unnamed register, and
`:put =expr` evaluates an expression the way `"=` does.

Somebody assembled this registry by pasting. The `import { container }`
line has ended up at the bottom, and it belongs on line 1. The
`export { container };` line is in the middle, and it belongs last.
Fix both without moving the cursor to either destination.

@keys
| `:0put a` | put `a` above the first line |
| `:$put a` | put `a` after the last line |
| `:12put a` | put `a` after line 12 |
| `:put =range(1, 5)` | put the numbers 1 to 5, one per line |
| `:let @a = 'text'` | write a register by hand |

@hints
Delete each line into a register, then put the register at the line
number you want. Two deletes, two `:put` commands, no travel.
--
`/tsyringe` Enter finds the import: `container` is on every line, so
search for something only the import has. `"add`, then `:0put a`.
--
`/^export` Enter, `"bdd`, `:$put b`.

@solution
    /tsyringe<Enter> "add :0put a<Enter>  /^export<Enter> "bdd :$put b<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

`:put` is the register command for when the destination is a line
number rather than a place your cursor happens to be, and it composes
with the whole Ex language of chapter 24. It also gives you a way to
write a register without yanking: `:let @a = 'text'` sets `a` directly,
and `:put =` turns any expression into lines.

That last pair is how chapter 21 edits macros. A macro is a register
with keystrokes in it. `"qp` puts it in the buffer as text, you fix the
text, `"qyy` picks it back up, or `:let @q = '...'` writes it outright.
Everything in this chapter applies to `q` the moment you record into it.

@start registry.ts
import { OrderRepository } from '../domain/order/order.repository';
import { OrderProvider } from '../domain/order/order.provider';
import { CustomerRepository } from '../domain/customer/customer.repository';
import { CustomerProvider } from '../domain/customer/customer.provider';
import { PaymentService } from '../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

container.register('OrderRepository', { useClass: OrderRepository });
container.register('OrderProvider', { useClass: OrderProvider });
export { container };
container.register('CustomerRepository', { useClass: CustomerRepository });
container.register('CustomerProvider', { useClass: CustomerProvider });
container.register('PaymentService', { useClass: PaymentService });
import { container } from 'tsyringe';

@expected registry.ts
import { container } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from '../domain/order/order.repository';
import { OrderProvider } from '../domain/order/order.provider';
import { CustomerRepository } from '../domain/customer/customer.repository';
import { CustomerProvider } from '../domain/customer/customer.provider';
import { PaymentService } from '../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

container.register('OrderRepository', { useClass: OrderRepository });
container.register('OrderProvider', { useClass: OrderProvider });
container.register('CustomerRepository', { useClass: CustomerRepository });
container.register('CustomerProvider', { useClass: CustomerProvider });
container.register('PaymentService', { useClass: PaymentService });
export { container };
